      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "80860855-b0bb-5344-aa2d-72bcd6ba3e02",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Medhatithi Gautama, Pāṇini, Gotama, Akṣapāda Gautama, Nagarjuna, Dignāga\n- Works: Nyāya Sūtras\n- Schools: Vaisheshika, Nyaya, Buddhist logic, Hindu philosophy\n- Concepts: Indian logic, anviksiki, atomism, inference, tetralemma, reason, epistemology, metaphysics\n\nFacts:\n- Indian logic developed from the anviksiki of Medhatithi Gautama around the 7th century BCE.\n- Indian logic also drew on the Sanskrit grammar rules of Pāṇini around the 5th century BCE.\n- The Vaisheshika school contributed to Indian logic through its analysis of atomism around the 2nd century BCE.\n- Gotama, founder of the Nyaya school of Hindu philosophy, developed an analysis of inference around the 2nd century.\n- Nagarjuna contributed the tetralemma to Indian logic around the 2nd century CE.\n- The Nyāya Sūtras is an ancient Indian Sanskrit text composed by Akṣapāda Gautama and is the foundational text of the Nyaya school of Hindu philosophy.\n- The Nyāya Sūtras consists of five books with two chapters in each book and a total of 528 aphoristic sutras on reason, logic, epistemology, and metaphysics.\n- Dignāga, who lived circa 480 to 540 CE, was an Indian Buddhist scholar and one of the Buddhist founders of Indian logic, also called hetu vidyā.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "7d193751-e8d8-5bb7-b8d8-d4da38dcd5a7",
        "text": "Page: Indian logic. Sentence: The development of Indian logic dates back to the \"anviksiki\" of Medhatithi Gautama (c. 7th century BCE) the Sanskrit grammar rules of Pāṇini (c. 5th century BCE); the Vaisheshika school's analysis of atomism (c. 2nd century BCE); the analysis of inference by Gotama (c. 2nd century), founder of the Nyaya school of Hindu philosophy; and the tetralemma of Nagarjuna (c. 2nd century CE).\nPage: Nyāya Sūtras. Sentence: The Nyāya Sūtras is an ancient Indian Sanskrit text composed by Akṣapāda Gautama , and the foundational text of the Nyaya school of Hindu philosophy.\nPage: Nyāya Sūtras. Sentence: The text consists of five books, with two chapters in each book, with a cumulative total of 528 aphoristic sutras, about rules of reason, logic, epistemology and metaphysics.\nPage: Dignāga. Sentence: Dignāga (c. 480 – c. 540 CE) was an Indian Buddhist scholar and one of the Buddhist founders of Indian logic (\"hetu vidyā\").",
